{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 1,
  "simulation_id": "sim-04",
  "member_id": "pearce-mark",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B017",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B005",
    "B024"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B005 to B017. Optical lattice clocks meet the test I would apply to a mature experimental achievement: a sharply defined physical architecture, decisive realization, and repeated independent performance across laboratories. The magic-wavelength approach created a durable new precision-measurement regime, with clear value for fundamental measurements as well as time-frequency metrology. Katori's originating concept and Ye's foundational experimental advances provide a compact attribution that is materially more defensible than sole credit for a large observatory collaboration.",
    "case_against": "The distinction between inventing and realizing the optical-lattice-clock method and the many subsequent advances in clock operation, comparison, and performance must remain exact. The proposed citation should not claim credit for all modern optical-clock capability or rely on a prospective redefinition of the second. Historical review is still needed to establish that the two named contributions are sufficiently distinct and foundational within that narrower formulation.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that the magic-wavelength lattice-clock architecture is mature through repeated independent demonstrations, while cautioning that later technical developments and eventual time-standard adoption are separate matters.",
        "response": "I agree with both parts of this assessment. From an instrument builder's perspective, independent reproduction is decisive evidence that an idea has become a reliable physical capability. It also supports a citation restricted to invention and realization, rather than to the collective later refinement of clock performance or an institutional standards decision."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson emphasizes the coherent Katori-Ye attribution and the already demonstrated capability of optical lattice clocks, contrasting it with quantum error correction's unresolved large-scale fault-tolerance threshold.",
        "response": "That comparison clarifies the maturity criterion. B017 does not require us to infer success from a future technical endpoint: the central architecture has already been realized broadly and has changed the available measurements. I would still require careful documentation of the respective roles of Katori and Ye before treating the two-person configuration as final."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson favors WMAP as a completed foundational empirical transformation, but identifies the difficulty of assigning three individual credits within a mission-scale collaboration.",
        "response": "WMAP remains an outstanding alternative, and its precision-cosmology impact is not in doubt. However, the same exact-credit concern that weakens IceCube also materially affects WMAP. B017 pairs comparable experimental durability with a more delimited inventive core and a more auditable attribution."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth regards the Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase as deeply established and fundamental, while questioning whether their distinct histories form one unified prize.",
        "response": "I share the view that B024 is an enduring intellectual achievement and retain it as an alternative. Its joint formulation, however, still requires resolving two distinct priority histories and possible omitted credit. B017 presently gives the committee the clearer single achievement, mature experimental record, and limited attribution."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical documentation establishes Katori and Ye as the two sufficiently distinct and indispensable contributors to the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks.",
      "How narrowly the citation must be bounded from later advances in optical-clock performance, comparison, and metrological implementation.",
      "Whether Francis Halzen's individual role can ultimately overcome the attribution problem for IceCube, which remains scientifically compelling as an alternative."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
